uman retry reuses, so a human or a janitor removes them.\n 4. **`:running` with no record.** Every 5 minutes, for each configured triage, worker and reviewer role, an open issue or PR that has the `:running` label but no running job (and none that ended in the last 10 minutes) gets one comment, `ard:executor:failed` and the label removed. It is a core-API list per role, not a search. It is skipped in dry-run, whose database never matches real labels, and for issues held with `ard:hold`.\n \n-Host log files older than 7 days are pruned hourly. The Reaper keeps running while the controller is paused.\n+Host log files older than 7 days are pruned hourly. The Reaper keeps running while the controller is paused. The claim gate checks daily token caps as well as dollar caps (see Budgets); a running job is never killed for either cap, only new claims are refused.\n \n Successful jobs clean up at close-out: the worktree is removed, a Kubernetes job's pod and per-job Secret are deleted (no Job object, no TTL), and logs stay 7 days.\n \n@@ -449,7 +449,7 @@ executor: local              # local | k8s; fixed at startup\n agent: claude                # default CLI for agent roles\n require_approval: true       # human adds ard:approved before a worker starts\n budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\n-#tokens_per_day: 40M        # optional per-role daily token default (40M, 1.5M, 500k or a plain integer); a role may set its own; unset = no token cap; recorded only, not enforced yet\n+#tokens_per_day: 40M        # optional per-role daily token default (40M, 1.5M, 500k or a plain integer); a role may set its own; unset = no token cap; enforced at the claim gate with the dollar caps\n max_budget_usd: 5            # per job; provisional, same recalibration\n global_max_open: 30          # open scanner issues across all scanners\n paused: false\n@@ -515,7 +515,7 @@ Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit\n - **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, including the HTML-entity (`&#35;12`, `&#x23;12`, `&num;12`) and backslash-escaped (`\\#12`) forms, which markdown renders as `#12`; \"fixed a typo in #12's test\" is left alone. Only ard's own `Closes #<issue>` on the worker PR closes an issue.\n - **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do; a literal is held.\n - **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n-- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Tokens are also recorded per role (`tokens_per_day`, optionally per role; unset means no token cap; nothing is enforced yet). Counted tokens are input + output + cache write; cache reads are excluded. A job that dies without reporting tokens is not counted: its close-out comment and cost-table row say \"tokens not reported\", and a role can overshoot its token cap by such runs. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n+- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Tokens are capped too (`tokens_per_day`, optionally per role; unset means no token cap), so a role on a model with no dollar price is capped by tokens alone. With U the tokens counted today, C the cap and W the role's largest reported job total, a candidate is refused when `U >= C` or `C - U < min(W, C)`: a role stops when what is left could not cover its worst job. Running jobs reserve nothing, and deterministic scanners are never checked. A job must pass both the dollar and the token check. If the token state cannot be read, the gate refuses with `budget state unreadable` and logs WARN `budget.read_failed`; a block is logged once a day as `budget.blocked`. The `budget` loop also posts one comment per token cap and UTC day, marked `<!-- ard:budget cap=tokens role=<kind>/<name> day=<YYYY-MM-DD> -->`, with numbers only. Counted tokens are input + output + cache write; cache reads are excluded. A job that dies without reporting tokens is not counted: its close-out comment and cost-table row say \"tokens not reported\", and a role can overshoot its token cap by such runs. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n - **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n - **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n - **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. The digest is a `digest` loop that posts one new comment a day on the status issue, titled \"daily digest for <date> (UTC)\", covering the previous complete UTC day: open-issue counts per `ard:` label, jobs run, failures (with a per-role split) and spend. A `digest` event written after the post is the once-per-day marker, so a restart does not repost; it holds numbers, label and role names only. A reached daily cap is a `budget_capped` event. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths. Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists.\ndiff --git a/internal